window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2021-05-11"]={"CSS-051-21":"Resolution No. CSS-051-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nACCEPTANCE OF CRIMINAL JUSTICE DISCOVERY REFORM GRANT & BUDGET\nMODIFICATION - DISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OFFICE\nWHEREAS, on January 1, 2020, the State of New York enacted sweeping Discovery and Bail Reform legislation that require District Attorney's Offices to obtain and turn over to defense, all evidence in any criminal case, regardless of the level of crime, within a very short period of time and eliminate bail for many offenses, and\nWHEREAS, this legislation requires the District Attorney's Office collect all items and information in its possession, including all information in the possession of any law enforcement agency, including reports, supplemental reports and investigative notes, 911 calls, radio transmissions, booking or surveillance video, photographs, body camera footage, witness and co-defendant statements, and the names of all law enforcement personnel and witnesses involved, for submittal to the defense within 15 days of arraigmnent for approximately 5,000 cases yearly, and\nWHEREAS, these discovery requirements significantly increase the burden on law enforcement agencies by requiring them to separate out, create a record of, and share every 911 call, radio transmission, booking or surveillance video and body cam footage for each arrest or appearance ticket issued within fifteen days of arraigmnent, and\nWHEREAS, this legislation also eliminated bail for many criminal offenses and created increased pre-trial supervision requirements, greatly reducing the number of people held in jail and increasing the responsibilities of Probation Departments, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services has recognized the extreme burden placed on District Attorney's Offices, Sheriffs Offices, Probation Departments and local law enforcement agencies outside of New York City as a result of these changes, and\nWHEREAS, funding in the amount of $40,000,000 has been set aside by the State of New York to support expenses related to the implementation of discovery and bail reforms that took effect January 1, 2020, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has been awarded $938,153 of these funds based on its proportionate share of criminal court arraigmnents statewide, to be distributed among and utilized by County agencies, and\nWHEREAS, funding will be provided to the following agencies that submitted requests to the District Attorney's Office, and whose requests were approved in full or in part, by the Division of Criminal Justice Services, and\nNiagara County District Attorney's Office  $318,381\nNiagara County Sheriffs Office  $288,726\nNiagara County Probation Department  $ 38,209\nNiagara Falls Police Department  $157,805\nLockport Police Department  $ 52,652\nNorth Tonawanda Police Department  $ 43,313\nTown of Niagara Police Department  $ 20,807\nLewiston Police Department  $ 13,164\nMiddleport Police Department  $ 5,096\nWHEREAS, the District Attorney has determined the need for additional staffing to address the significant increase in the workload resulting from the criminal justice mandates which should be addressed through the use of this funding, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Probation Department has identified critical public safety needs that include additional GPS monitoring equipment and related expenses which should be addressed with these funds, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has identified critical training needs for law enforcement personnel which should be addressed with these funds, and\nWHEREAS, the funds would be used to purchase a Use of Force Simulator which would benefit the entire county, as well as be used toward a training facility to be built on property currently owned by Niagara County in conjunction with grant funding, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara does hereby accept this award, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant award contract, the County Attorney will review the grant award contract for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and is hereby authorized to execute the grant award documents, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that effective May 10, 2021, one (1) permanent full time Assistant District Attorney position, Job Group ATT, District Attorney Level II, Step 1, $69,258 annually, be created and filled, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that effective May 10, 2021, one (1) permanent full time Assistant District Attorney position, Job Group ATT, District Attorney Level III, Step 1, $80,802 annually, be created and filled, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated in the 2021 operating budgets of the District Attorney, Sheriffs Office and Probation Department: INCREASE REVENUE:\nA.02.1165.000.43389.29  Other Public Safety-Discovery/Bail Reform  $431,508\nA.18.3140.000.43389.29  Other Public Safety-Discovery/Bail Reform  $ 9,740\nA.18.3989.302.43389.29  Other Public Safety-Discovery/Bail Reform  $ 11,900\nH.715.17.3197.000.43389.29 Other Public Safety-Discovery/Bail Reform  $288,725\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.02.1165.000.71010.00.xx Positions- Assistant District Attorney  $ 45,111\nA.02.1165.000. 71010.00.xx Positions- Assistant District Attorney  $ 52,630\nA.02.1165.000.74300.04  Reimb., DA Mileage, Sec 825 County Law  $  1,400\nA.02.1165.000.74400.09  Payments to Other Agencies  $292,837\nA.02.1165.000.78100.00  Retirement  $ 10,361\nA.02.1165.000.78200.00  FICA  $ 7,477\nA.02.1165.000.78300.00  Worker's Compensation  $ 2,570\nA.02.1165.000.78400.01  Healthcare  $ 17,222\nA.02.1165.000.78400.05  Insurance, HRA  $  1,134\nA.02.1165.000.78800.06  Flex 125  $  766\nA.18.3140.000.71060.00  Beeper Pay Expense  $ 4,000\nA.18.3140.000.72100.21  Machinery & Equipment, Law Enforcement  $  1,300\nA.18.3140.000. 74500.01  Contractual Expenses  $ 4,000\nA.18.3140.000.74375.06  Communications Postage, other  $  440\n.18.3989.302.72100.05  Machinery & Equipment, Computer Equipment  $ 2,400\nA.18.3989.302.74250.01  Office Expenses I Office Supplies  $ 4,000\nA.18.3989.302.74300.03  Reimbursements Travel, Mileage  $  250\nA.18.3989.302.74375.05  Communications Cellular Phone  $  600\nA.18.3989.302.74600.02  Professional Development Books & Subscriptions  $ 2,000\nA.18.3989.302.74600.03  Professional Development Training & Education  $ 1,500\nA.18.3989.302.74675.03  Services, Central Print Shop  $  150\nA.18.3989.302.74725.02  Services, Other Lab Services  $ 1,000\nH715.17.3197.000.72100.21 Law Enforcement Equipment  $196,610\nH715.17.3197.000.72400.00 Land Improvement Expense  $ 92,115\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-052-21":"Resolution No. CSS-052-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nABOLISH AND CREATE POSITION\nDISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OFFICE\nWHEREAS, the District Attorney's Office currently has a full time Court Assistant position #4758, Job Group 5, Step 8 at $20.77 per hour in the existing budget, and\nWHEREAS, due to the magnitude of changes made in the procedures of the office resulting from Criminal Justice Reform requirements, the operations of the support staff within the District Attorney's Office have been reevaluated, and\nWHEREAS, it has been determined that a Senior Court Assistant Position is needed in the County Court Office to supervise staff training and manage the criminal case management system to aid with meeting electronic Discovery requirement, and\nWHEREAS, the District Attorney has the funds available in the 2021 operating budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that effective May 10, 2021, a Senior Court Assistant position, Job Group VI, $19.64 - $22.52 per hour, be created and filled, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Court Assistant position, #4758, Job Group V, $20.77 per hour, be abolished pending Civils Service Exam results for the Senior Court Assistant position, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated in the District Attorney's 2021 operating budget: DECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.02.1165.000.71010.00.04758  Positions-Court Assistant  $24,716\nA.02.1165.000.71010.00.00047  Positions-Assistant District Attorney  631\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.02.1165.000.71010.00.xxxxx  Positions-Sr. DA Court Assistant  $25,347\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-053-21":"Resolution No. CSS-053-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nACCEPTANCE OF GUN INVOLVED VIOLENCE ELIMINATION INITIATIVE GRANT\nWHEREAS, in 2014 the Division of Criminal Justice Services initiated a gun violence eradication initiative to target the rising incidents of gun involved violence across the State of New York and specifically the seventeen counties that account for the vast majority of crime outside of the City of New York which includes the County of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, the GIVE (Gun Involved Violence Elimination) Initiative is a statewide comprehensive strategy that focuses exclusively on reducing shootings and homicides by promoting integrated, evidence based strategies, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and the Niagara County Probation Department, in conjunction with the Niagara Falls Police Department, applied for the renewal of funding under the 2021-2022 GIVE Initiative, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office and the Niagara County Probation Department have received notification that funding has been awarded in the amounts of $167,405 and $66,720 respectively, for the period of July 1, 2021 through June 30, 2022, with no required match from Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the District Attorney's Office has an Assistant District Attorney position #4771, which is currently funded under this program and will remain coterminous with grant funding, and\nWHEREAS, revenue and appropriation figures for said program were calculated and are contained within the District Attorney's Office and Probation Department budgets for fiscal year 2021, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara does hereby accept this award, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant award contract, the County Attorney will review the grant award contract for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and is hereby authorized to execute the grant award documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-054-21":"Resolution No. CSS-054-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION AND ACCEPT STATE FARM INSURANCE GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office was notified by State Farm Insurance that they are the recipient of a grant through their Good Neighbor Citizenship Grant in the amount of $13,750, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are designated to be used to keep kids safe in Niagara County by addressing young driver safety, bike safety and child car seat safety, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to modify revenue and appropriations in the 2021 Budget in order to utilize the funding, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this grant, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be made:\nINCREASE REVENEUE:\nCM.17.3989.303 42705.00  Gifts and Donations Revenue  $13,750\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCM.17.3989.303 74600.03  Training and Education  $13,750\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-055-21":"Resolution No. CSS-055-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE-BUDGET MODIFICATION\nBUDGET MODIFICATION AND ACCEPT EXPLOSIVE\nDETECTION CANINE TEAM GRANT PROGRAM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded a grant in the amount of $15,000 from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services to protect Niagara County residents and infrastructure from attacks involving explosive devices for the period April 1, 2021 through August 31, 2022,and\nWHEREAS, the grant will allow the Niagara County Sheriffs Office to purchase equipment and provide training to enhance explosive detection canine team capabilities, and\nWHEREAS, the revenue and equal expenses must be added to the 2021 Budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant, the County Attorney will review the grant for approval to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the grant agreement, and be it further\nRESOLVED that the following budget modifications be made:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $15,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 72100.05  Computer Equipment  $ 3,270\nA.17.3645.000 74600.03  Training and Education  4,000\nA.17.3645.000 74750.05  Law Enforcement Supplies  7,730\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-056-21":"Resolution No. CSS-056-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE-BUDGET MODIFICATION\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -ACCEPT FY19 TACTICAL TEAM TARGETED GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded a grant in the amount of $75,000 from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services to enhance the capabilities and safety of the Emergency Response Team for the performance period April l, 2021 through August 31, 2022, and\nWHEREAS, the grant will allow the Niagara County Sheriffs Office to purchase Emergency Response Team equipment and training that would facilitate the team's advancement of Tactical Team Capabilities; consistent training as a team unit with necessary tactical equipment will mitigate risk to team members, and\nWHEREAS, the 2021 budget will need to be modified to allow for the spending of this grant, now therefore, be it\nRESOLVED that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the agreement and be it further\nRESOLVED that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE\nA.17.3645.000.44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $75,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS\nA.17.3645.000 71050.00  Overtime Expense  $ 6,000\nA.17 .3645.000 72100.21  Law Enforcement Equipment  43,500\nA.17.3645.000 74500.01  Contractual Expenses  2,000\nA.17.3645.000 74750.05  Law Enforcement Supplies  23,500\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-057-21":"Resolution No. CSS-057-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT GUN INVOLVED VIOLENCE ELIMINATION (GIVE) GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that a grant in the amount of $87,049 is being awarded to the Sheriffs Office through the Gun Involved Violence Elimination (GIVE) Grant for the period July 1, 2021 through June 30, 2022, and\nWHEREAS, the GIVE grant funds a Correction Officer position #10442 and travel for DCJS sponsored trainings and conferences, and\nWHEREAS, the revenue and equal expenses are already in the 2021 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant, the County Attorney will review the grant for approval to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this grant.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-058-21":"Resolution No. CSS-058-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE-BUDGET MODIFICATION\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -ACCEPT FY19 CRITICAL INFRASTRUCTURE GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded a Critical Infrastructure grant in the amount of $50,000 from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services to for the performance period April 1, 2021 through August 31, 2022, and\nWHEREAS, the grant will allow the Niagara County Sheriffs Office to update and enhance the video security system at the Niagara County 911 Dispatch Center/Public Safety Training Facility, and\nWHEREAS, the 2021 budget will need to be modified to allow for the spending of this grant, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the agreement, and be it further\nRESOLVED that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE\nA.17.3645.000.44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $50,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS\nA.17.3645.000 72100.05  Computer Equipment  $50,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-059-21":"Resolution No. CSS-059-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nNIAGARA FASHION MALL COMMUNICATIONS SITE CHANGE ORDER NO. 2\nWHEREAS, on April 30, 2020, pursuant to a sealed bid, the County of Niagara entered into a contract for Radio Site Development at Fashion Outlets Mall of Niagara Falls to Cellectric Electrical, LLC, 6615 Towpath Road, E. Syracuse, New York 13057 for a contract amount of$267,300.00, and\nWHEREAS, on August 4, 2020, by resolution CSS-037-20 a change order in the amount of $29,855.94 was\napproved and authorized for Radio Site Development at Fashion Outlets Mall of Niagara Falls to Cellectric Electrical, LLC, 6615 Towpath Road, E. Syracuse, New York 13057 making the total contract $297,155.94, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 2 to decrease the contract by $156,155.94 for Radio Site Development at Fashion Outlets Mall of Niagara Falls to Cellectric Electrical, LLC, for a revised contract amount of $141,000.00 to Cellectric Electrical, LLC, 6615 Towpath Road, E. Syracuse, New York 13057, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-060-21":"Resolution No. CSS-060-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\n2021 BUDGET MODIFICATIONS FOR EMERGENCY SERVICES-COVID 19 SERVICES\nWHEREAS, the Emergency Services Office provides services to protect the safety of the citizens of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the COVID-19 pandemic response has required modifications to the budget to cover expenses for continued vaccinations and testing, as the situation is evolving, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following 2021 budget modification be effectuated effective immediately: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.40599.00  $146,990\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.19.3640.000 72100.14  Misc. Equipment  $100,000\nA.19.3640.000 74800.10  Misc. Equipment Under $500  $ 46,990\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-061-21":"Resolution No. CSS-061-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\n2021 BUDGET MODIFICATIONS FOR EMERGENCY SERVICES\nTRANSIT DRIVE-IN PROPERTY REP AIR\nWHEREAS, the Emergency Services Office has been providing services to protect the safety of the citizens of Niagara County during the COVID-19 pandemic by assisting with the Niagara County Department of Health vaccine drive-up PODs (Point of Dispensing) at the Transit Drive-In several times over the past few months, and\nWHEREAS, the Transit Drive-In has provided use of the space at no cost to the County for all vaccine PODs and the large number of vehicles has caused some damage to the stoned parking lots, and\nWHEREAS, the repair of the parking lots would cause an undue burden on the property owner, Niagara County will supply the required stone, transport and place it down on the lots at a cost of $3,010 to the Niagara County Highway Department with the monies that have been allocated in the budget for COVID-19 pandemic expenses, and\nWHEREAS, a budgetary modification is necessary to provide the Highway Department the cost of said work from the COVID-19 pandemic expenses in the budget of the Emergency Services Office, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following 2021 budget modification be effectuated effective immediately: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA40599.00  $3,010.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.19.3640.000 74800.06  Repairs & Maintenance  $3,010.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-062-21":"Resolution No. CSS-062-21\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nBUDGET MODIFICATION OF 2020 HAZARDOUS MATERIALS\nEMERGENCY PREPAREDNESS (HMEP) GRANT- HOMELAND SECURITY GRANTS\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has accepted the 2020 Hazardous Materials Emergency Preparedness (HMEP) grant through the Department of Homeland Security for the period of 4/1/21 to 7 /31/22, at some cost to the County (Resolution CSS-047-21), and\nWHEREAS, the grant for HMEP 2020 was awarded and budgeted in the amount of $5,000, the budget must be modified to include the matching funds of $1,000 (20% match) and modify revenue to $4,000 (80% match),\nnow, therefore be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated immediately:\nINCREASE APPROPIA TION FUND BALANCE - ASSIGNED:\nA.40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $1,000.00\nDECREASE REVENUE:HMEP20:\nA.19.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $1,000.00\nProject: 19GRTHMEP20\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CW-007-21":"Resolution No. CW-007-21\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nLEASE AGREEMENT WITH SINATRA AND COMPANY\nAND NIAGARA COUNTY PUBLIC DEFENDER'S OFFICE\nWHEREAS, the New York State Office of Indigent Legal Services (ILS) and Niagara County (Contractor) have entered into a Master Contract For Grants (Contract) to promulgate the application of the Hurrell-Harring Settlement with the Niagara County Public Defender's Office, and\nWHEREAS, under Section IV, D (l)(e) \"A rental charge to the Master Contract for a piece of Property owned by the Contractor shall not be allowed\", and\nWHEREAS, the Contractor is Niagara County, the Niagara County Public Defender's must lease property that is not owned by Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has issued a Request For Proposal (RFP) and reviewed the RFP on February 28, 2020 for the leasing of property for the Niagara County Public Defender's Office within 1 mile of the Niagara County Court House, and\nWHEREAS, Sinatra and Company was the successful bidder for RFP 2020-26, however due to the COVID-19 pandemic and its uncertainty, the bid had not yet been awarded, and\nWHEREAS, the Public Defender's Office is now looking to move forward with awarding RFP 2020-26 to accommodate its needs, and\nWHEREAS, a year has lapsed since the bid submittal, so both bidders were provided an opportunity to update their bids should they wish to do so, and\nWHEREAS, Sinatra and Company offered an updated lease for 5,750 square feet of office space located at 170 East Avenue, Lockport, NY, for a five (5) year term at a descending annual rent of: Year 1: $88,992.42, Year 2: $88,150.92, Year 3: $86,098.46, Year 4: $84,096.06, and Year 5: $82,142.50 with a renewal option oftwo(2) five (5) year terms, which the County wishes to accept, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County enter into the above described lease with Sinatra and Company for office space at 170 East Avenue, Lockport, NY, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-016-21":"Resolution No. ED-016-21\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAUTHORIZATION TO SUBMIT A GRANT APPLICATION TO THE\nNEW YORK STATE OFFICE OF COMMUNITY RENEWAL FOR\nBLOCK GRANT FUNDING THROUGH THE CORONA VIRUS AID, RELIEF,\nAND ECONOMIC SECURITIES ACT TO IMPLEMENT THE\nNIAGARA ORLEANS REGIONAL ALLIANCE WORKPLAN\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Orleans Regional Alliance (NORA) is a collaboration between Niagara and Orleans Counties designed to bring broadband access to areas within the counties that are currently unserved or underserved by broadband, and\nWHEREAS, NORA has developed a Workplan to extend broadband service to those areas of need, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Office of Community Renewal (the \"OCR\") through the Coronavirus Aid, Relief, and Economic Securities Act (\"CARES\") has made funds available for public service or infrastructure projects designed to ensure that families in underserved communities have Wi-fi access for remote education and telehealth services through new and expanded broadband, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County (\"the County\") is an eligible applicant for CARES funding and implementation of the NORA Workplan is consistent with the goals of said funding as a CDBG - funded Project (the \"Project\"), and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Industrial Development Agency (NCIDA) has experience administering OCR grants secured by Niagara County for the implementation CDBG projects, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has held a public hearing to obtain citizens' views regarding the CDBG program as administered by OCR and the Project, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature is hereby authorized to submit a grant application on behalf of Niagara County to the OCR to support the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman is hereby authorized to execute a grant agreement between the County and the OCR and all related documents associated with the OCR grant, including entering into a grant agreement with the NCIDA for the implementation of the Project and administration of the OCR grant, all such documents to be subject to review and approval by the County Attorney, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman is hereby designated as the Certifying Officer responsible for all activities associated with the federal environmental review process to be completed in conjunction with the Project.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-045-21":"Resolution No. IF-045-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nHOUSEHOLD HAZARDOUS WASTE EVENTS CONTRACT AW ARD\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from bidders for the 2021-2024 Household Hazardous Waste Events, and\nWHEREAS, only one (!) vendor submitted a proposal for review, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Household Hazardous Waste Event contract for the period of May 1, 2021 through December 31, 2024, be awarded to Environmental Services Group, 177 Wales Avenue, Tonawanda, NY 14150, for an annual contract amount not to exceed $75,000, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-046-21":"Resolution No. IF-046-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA AND\nTOWN OF LOCKPORT RECREATION COMMISSION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Golf Course has made a proposal to the Town of Lockport to provide golf lessons at its premises for the 2021 golf season, and\nWHEREAS, the proposal has been reviewed by the Town of Lockport Commission and is in agreement with moving forward with this agreement, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Golf Course will accept passes as provided through the Town of Lockport newsletter, for discounts in the amount of three dollars off the price of Junior League golf lessons, and for a discount in the amount of fifteen dollars off the package price of the Junior Clinic lessons package, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Lockport will reimburse the Niagara County Golf Course in the amount coinciding with the passes collected with the name and address of the town participant, and date of lesson, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chair of the County Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the License Agreement between the County of Niagara and Town of Lockport Recreation Commission.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-047-21":"Resolution No. IF-047-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA\nAND THE OLCOTT LION'S CLUB\nWHEREAS, the Olcott Lions Club has requested that the County of Niagara grant them permission to use the north section of Krull Park on May 23, 2021 for the purpose of holding the annual charity event known as the Lion's Club Polar Swim for Sight, and\nWHEREAS, this event has been successful for many years in attracting thousands of visitors from across Niagara County and Greater Western New York in order to raise money for Lion's Club sight and other charity programs, and\nWHEREAS, the Lion's Club has also raised money through this event for improvements which benefit Krull Park, the surrounding community and Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, it is the wish of the Olcott Lion's Club to hold the Lion's Club Polar Bear Swim for Sight on May 23,2021,and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Olcott Lion's Club, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the Olcott Lion's Club, as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature is hereby authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-048-21":"Resolution No. IF-048-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA AND\nTHE ALZHEIMER'S ASSOCIATION\nWHEREAS, the Alzheimer's Association has requested that the County of Niagara grant them permission to use a portion of Krull Park for the purpose of holding a fundraiser, and\nWHEREAS, it is the wish of the Alzheimer's Association to hold the fundraiser on June 19, 2021, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Alzheimer's Association, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the license agreement between the County of Niagara and the Alzheimer's Association, the County Attorney will review said Agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chair of the County Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the License Agreement between the County of Niagara and the Alzheimer's Association.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-049-21":"Resolution No. IF-049-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA\nAND THE OLCOTT FIRE COMP ANY SOFTBALL LEAGUE\nWHEREAS, the Olcott Fire Company Softball League has requested that the County of Niagara grant them permission to use existing ball fields in an area situated in the eastern portion of Krull Park for the purpose of softball games, and\nWHEREAS, this program benefits the residents of Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, such program is operated on a not-for-profit basis, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara to enter into a formal agreement with the Olcott Fire Company Softball League, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the Olcott Fire Company Softball League, as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the county Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the License Agreement between the County of Niagara and the Olcott Fire Company Softball League.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-050-21":"Resolution No. IF-050-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA\nAND THE LIGHTHOUSE CLASSIC SOFTBALL LEAGUE\nWHEREAS, Lighthouse Classic Softball League has requested that the County of Niagara grant them permission to use existing ball fields in an area situated in the eastern portion of Krull Park for the purpose of softball games, and\nWHEREAS, this program benefits the residents of Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, such program is operated on a not-for-profit basis, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara to enter into a formal agreement with the Lighthouse Classic Softball League, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the Lighthouse Classic Softball League, as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the county Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the License Agreement between the County of Niagara and the Lighthouse Classic Softball League.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-051-21":"Resolution No. IF-051-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nRESCIND RESOLUTION IF-027-21- AW ARD OF CONTRACT FOR\nCOUNTY ROAD PAINTING AND STRIPING\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-027-21, dated March 23, 2021, awarded the contract to the lowest responsible bidder, Seneca Pavement Marking, Inc., and\nWHEREAS, there was a typo in the text of the resolution, that awarded the contract to Atlantic Pavement Marking, Inc. and not to Seneca Pavement Marking, Inc. as the bids depicted, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to rescind Resolution IF-027-21, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Resolution IF-027-21 be rescinded, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-052-21":"Resolution No. IF-052-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAW ARD OF CONTRACT\nCOUNTY ROAD PAINTING AND STRIPING\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works, Division of Highways, Bridges & Structures has prepared specifications and the Purchasing Department advertised for bids for painting traffic lines on County roads, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in the 2021 budget of the Highways, Bridges & Structures Division of the Department of Public Works, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on March 11, 2021 and tabulated below:\nPrice Per Mile  Price Per Mile\nWaterborne Paint Application  Of Centerline  OfEdgeLine\nSeneca Pavement Marking Inc.  $303.00  $176.00\n3526 Watkins Road\nHorseheads, NY 14854\nAccent Stripe, Inc.  $317.90  $197.90\n3275 N. Benzing Road\nOrchard Park, NY 14127\nAtlantic Pavement Marking Inc.  $379.99  $229.99\n15 Industrial Road\nProspect, CT 06712\nand\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the low bid of Seneca Pavement Marking, Inc., 3526 Watkins Road, Horseheads, NY 14854, for painting traffic lines on County roads be accepted as the lowest responsible bid meeting specifications, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-053-21":"Resolution No. IF-053-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAW ARD OF CONTRACT - BEAR RIDGE ROAD CUL VERT REHABILITATION\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Bear Ridge Road Culvert Rehabilitation Project, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on April 22, 2021 as tabulated below:\nI.  Concrete Applied Technologies Corp. (CATCO)  $227,749.00\n1266 Townline Road\nAlden, NY 14004\n2.  4th Generation Construction  $258,675.00\n5650 Simmons Avenue\nNiagara Falls, NY 14304\n3.  Union Concrete Construction Corp.  $289,656.50\nPO Box 410\nWest Seneca, NY 14224\n4.  Accadia Site Contracting  $294,506.00\n5636 Transit Road\nDepew, NY 14043\n5.  Northspur Construction  $385,787.21\n157 Garrison Road\nBuffalo, NY 14221\nand\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: INCREASE REVENUE:\nH652.15.5197.000 43501.00  Consol Highway Aid Revenue  $150,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH652.15.5 l 97.000 72600.02  Bridges  $150,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Bear Ridge Road Culvert Rehabilitation Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, Concrete Applied Technologies Corp., d/b/a CATCO, 1266 Townline Road, Alden, NY 14004, in the amount of$227,749.000, plus any other construction incidentals that may arise, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-054-21":"Resolution No. IF-054-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nANGELO DELSIGNORE CIVIC BUILDING\nCURTAIN WALL RENOVATION FINAL PAYMENT\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-023-20, dated February 18, 2020, the Legislature awarded the contract for the Angelo DelSignore Civic Building Curtain Wall Renovation Project to Huber Construction Inc., 136 Taylor Drive, Depew, NY 14043, for a contract amount of$510,000, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-103-20, dated September 16, 2020, authorized Change Order No. 1 to increase the contract in the amount of $28,559 for glass upgrades, deletion of blinds and temporary doors, and revisions to data drops, for a revised contract amount of$538,559, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-121-20, dated November 17, 2020, authorized Change Order No. 2 - Final Payment, for a reduction to the contract in the amount of $10,670.95, due to adjustments in contract quantities, for a revised contract amount of$527,888.05, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-005-21, dated January 19, 2020, authorized Change Order No. 3 - Time Extension, to amend the contract date to February 18, 2022, and\nWHEREAS, the contract work and removal of construction debris has been completed as per specifications and drawings, for the amount of $527,888.05, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 4-Final Payment is authorized to be processed for the Angelo DelSignore Civic Building Curtain Wall Renovation Project, for a contract amount of $527,888.05, to Huber Construction Inc., 136 Taylor Drive, Depew, NY 14043, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-055-21":"Resolution No. IF-055-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nREHABILITATION OF NORTH CANAL ROAD\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 1 - FINAL\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-041-20, dated March 10, 2020, the Legislature awarded the contract for the Rehabilitation ofNorth Canal Road to Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, for a contract amount of$2,272,103.00, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to decrease the contract in the amount of $0.16 due to final quantities adjustments, for a revised contract amount of $2,272, 102.84, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1-Final to decrease the contract by $0.16 for the Rehabilitation of North Canal Road, for a revised contract amount of $2,272, I 02.84, to Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-056-21":"Resolution No. IF-056-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nBEAR RIDGE ROAD BRIDGE OVER AN UNNAMED STREAM\nCONSULTANT AMENDMENT NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-101-20, dated September 15, 2020, authorized the contract for consultant services to assist the County with the Bear Ridge Road Bridge over an Unnamed Stream Project to C&S Engineers, Inc., 499 Col. Eileen Collins Blvd., Syracuse, NY 13212, for a fee not to exceed $99,000, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in the amount of $83,000 for construction inspection and construction administration, for a revised contract amount of $182,000, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: INCREASE REVENUE:\nH713.15.5197.000 43501.00  Consol Hwy Aid Rev  $33,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH713.15.5197.000 72600.02  Bear Ridge Road Bridge  $33,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the consulting services contract with C&S Engineers, Inc., 499 Col. Eileen Collins Blvd., Syracuse, NY 13212, for the Bear Ridge Road Bridge over an Unnamed Stream Project, be amended by $83,000 for construction inspection and construction administration, for a total fee not to exceed $182,000, and\nbe it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-057-21":"Resolution No. IF-057-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nAKRON ROAD PAVEMENT REHABILITATION\nFEDERAL AID LOCAL PROJECT AGREEMENT\nSUPPLEMENTAL AGREEMENT NO. 1\nWHEREAS, the Akron Road Pavement Rehabilitation Project, town of Lockport, Niagara County, is eligible for funding under Title 23 US Code, as amended, that calls for the apportionment of the costs of such program to be borne at the ratio of 80% federal funds and 20% non-federal funds, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara desires to advance the Project by making a commitment of 100% of the Non-Federal share of the costs of the Engineering (Design I-VI) and Construction/Construction Inspection phases of the project, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby approves the above-subject project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby authorizes the County of Niagara to pay in the first instance 100% of the federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the Engineering (Design I-VI) and Construction/Construction Inspection phases of the Project or portions thereof, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE:\nH701.15.5112.000 44597.01  Federal Aid Cap Const Hwy  $942,400\nH701.15.5112.000 43591.00  State Aid Cap Const Hwy  30,000\nH701.15.5112.000 43501.00  Consol Hwy Aid Revenue  205,600\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED APPROPRIATIONS:\nH701.15.5112.000 72600.01  Infrastructure Roads  $1,178,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the sum of $1,780,000 is hereby appropriated in account H701.15.5112.000 72600.01 and made available to cover the cost of participation in the above phases of the project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that in the event the amount required to pay the full federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the project's Engineering (Design I-VI) and Construction/Construction Inspection phases exceeds the amount appropriated above, the County of Niagara shall convene its Legislature as soon as possible to appropriate said excess amount immediately upon the notification by the New York State Department of Transportation, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chair of the Legislature of the County of Niagara be, and hereby is, authorized to execute all necessary agreements, certifications, or reimbursement requests for Federal Aid on behalf of the County of Niagara with the New York State Department of Transportation, in connection with the advancement or approval of the Project providing for the administration of the Project and the Municipality's first instance funding of Project costs and permanent funding of the local share of Federal Aid eligible Project costs and all Project costs within appropriations therefor that are not so eligible, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that a certified copy of this Resolution be filed with the New York State Commissioner of Transportation by attaching it to any necessary Agreement in connection with the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this Resolution shall take effect immediately.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-058-21":"Resolution No. IF-058-21\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nBRIDGE WASHING AND SEALING OF VARIO US BRIDGES: TOWNS OF HARTLAND,\nLOCKPORT, NEWFANE, NIAGARA, PENDLETON, PORTER, ROYALTON, SOMERSET,\nWHEATFIELD, AND WILSON\nFEDERAL AID LOCAL PROJECT AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, the Bridge Washing and Sealing of Various Bridges in the Towns of Hartland, Lockport, Newfane, Niagara, Pendleton, Porter, Royalton, Somerset, Wheatfield, and Wilson Project, Niagara County, PIN 5763.64 (the \"Project\"), is eligible for funding under Title 23 US Code, as amended, that calls for the apportionment of the costs of such program to be borne at the ratio of 80% federal funds and 20% non-federal funds, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara desires to advance the Project by making a commitment of 100% of the non-federal share of the costs of the Construction/Construction Inspection phases of the project, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby approves the above-subject project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby authorizes the County of Niagara to pay in the first instance 100% of the federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the Construction/Inspection phases of the Project or portions thereof, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to reflect the funding levels set forth by Schedule A in the agreement: INCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE:\nD.15.5120.000 44597.02  Cap Proj Hwy Planning/Const  $138,000\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED APPROPRIATIONS:\nD.15.5120.000 74800.06  Bridge Maintenance  $138,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that in the event the amount required to pay the full federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the project's Construction/Inspection phases exceeds the amount appropriated above, the County of Niagara shall convene its Legislature as soon as possible to appropriate said excess amount immediately upon the notification by the New York State Department of Transportation, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chair of the Legislature of the County of Niagara be, and hereby is, authorized to execute all necessary agreements, certifications, or reimbursement requests for Federal Aid on behalf of the County of Niagara with the New York State Department of Transportation, in connection with the advancement or approval of the Project providing for the administration of the Project and the Municipality's first instance funding of Project costs and permanent funding of the local share of Federal Aid eligible Project costs and all Project costs within appropriations therefor that are not so eligible, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that a certified copy of this Resolution be filed with the New York State Commissioner of Transportation by attaching it to any necessary Agreement in connection with the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this Resolution shall take effect immediately.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-019-21":"Resolution No. IL-019-21\nFrom:  Legislators David E. Godfrey, Michael A. Hill, Rebecca J. Wydysh, Richard L. Andres, Randy R.\nBradt, Jesse P. Gooch, William J. Collins, Anthony J. Nemi, John Syracuse and Irene M. Myers.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nADOPTION OF A LOCAL LAW TO ADOPT CERTAIN BENEFITS UNDER\u00a7 458-b OF THE\nREAL PROPERTY TAX LAW OF THE STATE OF NEW YORK AUTHORIZING\nAN ALTERNATIVE TAX EXEMPTION FOR COLD WAR VETERANS\nBE CHANGED FROM 10% OPTION TO 15% OPTION\nWHEREAS, Legislator David E. Godfrey, et al. recommend the adoption of the following Local Law: A Local Law To Adopt Certain Benefits Under \u00a7 458-B of the Real Property Tax Law of the State of New York Authorizing an Alternative Tax Exemption for Cold War Veterans be Changed From 10% Option to 15% Option; and\nWHEREAS, a public hearing was held on May 4, 2021 at 5:30 p.m. in the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, Lockport, New York, on said Local Law, and\nWHEREAS,  no one  appeared to speak on said Local Law, and\nWHEREAS, _O_ amendments(s) was (were) made to said Local Law, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a Local Law to adopt certain benefits under\u00a7 458-b of the Real Property Tax Law of the State of New York authorizing an alternative tax exemption for Cold War Veterans,\nBe it enacted by the County Legislature of the County of Niagara as follows:\nSection I.  Pursuant to subsection 2(a) of\u00a7 458-b of the Real Property Tax Law of the State of New\nYork, the County of Niagara hereby adopts the qualifying residential real property exemption under subsection 2(a)(ii) of\u00a7 458-b of the Real Property Tax Law of the State of New York, that is, an exemption in the amount of fifteen percent of the assessed value of such property, provided however, that such exemption, as permitted by subsection 2(c)(iii), shall not exceed $15,000 or the product of $15,000 multiplied by the latest state equalization rate of such property's assessing unit, such exemption to be subject to the definitions, limitations and requirements of\u00a7 458-b.\nSection 2.  Pursuant to subsection 2(b) of\u00a7 458-b of the Real Property Tax Law of the State of New\nYork, the County of Niagara hereby adopts the qualifying residential real property exemption under subsection 2(b) of\u00a7 458-b of the Real Property Tax Law of the State of New York, that is, an exemption in the amount equal to the extent of the product of the assessed value of such property, multiplied by fifty percent of the Cold War veteran disability rating; provided, however, that such exemption, as permitted by subsection 2( c )(iii) shall not exceed fifty thousand dollars or the product of fifty thousand dollars multiplied by the latest state equalization rate for the assessing unit.\nSection 3.  This Local Law shall take immediately upon its filing with the Secretary of State and\nshall be applicable to all assessment rolls prepared pursuant to the first taxable status date occurring on or after the effective date of this local law.\nMoved by Godfrey, seconded by Syracuse, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Godfrey, seconded by Syracuse.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-020-21":"Resolution No. IL-020-21\nFrom:  Legislators David E. Godfrey, Michael A. Hill, Rebecca J. Wydysh, Randy R. Bradt, Jesse P. Gooch,\nWilliam J. Collins, Anthony J. Nemi, John Syracuse, Irene M. Myers and Richard L. Andres.\nDated: May 11, 2021\nA LOCAL LAW ADOPTING A PILOT YOUTH DEER HUNTING PROGRAM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature presents in writing the following proposed Local Law: A Local Law of the County of Niagara, New York, a local law adopting a pilot youth deer hunting program.\nBe it enacted by the County Legislature of the County of Niagara as follows:\nA new Niagara County Local Law is hereby enacted to read as follows:\nA LOCAL LAW ADOPTING, A PILOT YOUTH DEER HUNTING PROGRAM.\nSection 1. Purpose\nConsistent with the provisions of Environmental Conservation Law Section 11-0935, this Local Law is\nadopted to authorize the expansion of youth hunting and allow licensed hunters ages 12 or 13 to hunt deer with a crossbow, shotgun or muzzle-loading firearm when accompanied by a parent, legal guardian, or mentor. Section 2. Authority This Local Law is adopted under the authority granted by: 1. Article IX of the New York State Constitution, Section 2[c] 2. New York Municipal Home Rule Law, Section 10; and 3. New York Environmental Conservation Law Section 11-0935 Section 3. Definitions\n1.  \"Eligible area\" means any county within the State of New York that has enacted a local law\nauthorizing participation in a youth hunting pilot program, and has notified the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation of such participation.\n2.  \"Hunting license holder\" means a person who has successfully completed a hunter\neducation/safety course, and currently holds a valid hunting license issues by the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation.\n3.  \"Legal guardian\" means a person legally responsible for a minor participating in the Pilot\nYouth Deer Hunting Program who currently holds a valid hunting license issued by the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation and has at least three years of prior experience hunting deer as a hunting license holder.\n4.  \"Mentor\" means a person who (a) currently holds a valid hunting license issues by the New\nYork State Department of Environmental Conservation, (b )is twenty-one years of age or older, ( c) has at least three years of prior experience hunting deer as a hunting license holder, and ( d) has been designated in writing by a minor's parent or legal guardian on a form prescribed by the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation to serve as a mentor to said minor for purposes of the Pilot Youth Deer Hunting Program.\n5.  \"Minor\" means a youth twelve or thirteen years of age who currently holds a valid hunting\nlicense issued by the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation to hunt deer with a crossbow, shotgun or muzzle-loading firearm within an eligible area.\n6.  \"Parent\" means a person who currently holds a valid hunting license issued by the New York\nState Department of Environmental Conservation, has at least three years of prior experience hunting deer as a hunting license holder, and is the parent of the minor participating in the pilot Youth Deer Hunting Program.\n7.  \"Physical control\" means that the physical proximity of a minor to a parent, legal guardian or\nmentor and is such that the parent, legal guardian or mentor is (a) reasonably able to issue verbal directions and instructions, (b) maintain constant visual contact, and ( c) otherwise able to provide guidance and supervision to the minor. Section 4. Opt-In Authorization The youth hunting opportunities authorized by Environmental Conservation Law Section 11-093 5 shall be applicable within the boundaries of Niagara County upon (a) the adoption of this Local Law by the Legislatures for the County of Niagara, State of New York, and (b) notice provided to the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation of the adoption of this Local Law. Niagara County hereby elects to participate in a Pilot Youth Deer Hunting Program to allow minors ages 12 or 13 to hunt deer with a crossbow, shotgun or muzzle-loading firearm when accompanied by a parent, legal guardian or mentor. Section 5. Eligibility and Requirements I. A minor aged 12 or 13 may hunt deer with a crossbow, shotgun or muzzle-loading firearm within the County of Niagara when accompanied by, and is under the physical control of, a parent, legal guardian or mentor as those terms are defined by Section 3 of this Local Law. 2. A minor participating in the Pilot Youth Deer Hunting Program in an eligible area must be in possession of a valid New York State hunting license and valid tags, as appropriate, while afield at all times. The accompanying parent, legal guardian or mentor must also be in possession of a valid New York State hunting license at all times when supervising a minor participating in the Pilot Youth Deer Hunting Program. 3. A minor and the accompanying parent, legal guardian or mentor must display either a minimum total of two hundred fifty square inches of solid fluorescent orange or pink or patterned fluorescent orange or pink consisting of no less than fifty percent fluorescent orange or pink material worn above the waist and visible from all directions, or a hat or cap with no less than fifty percent of the exterior consisting of solid fluorescent orange or pink material and visible from all directions. 4. A minor aged 12 or 13 participating in the Pilot Youth Deer Hunting Program must remain at ground level at all times when hunting deer with a crossbow, shotgun or muzzle-loading firearm. 5. A minor participating in the Pilot Youth Deer Hunting Program must remain within the physical proximity of a parent, legal guardian or mentor which means they must be capable of received verbal directions and instructions, and be in constant visual contact with the supervising parent, legal guardian or mentor at all times. 6. A minor participating in the Pilot Youth Deer Hunting Program must abide by all other federal, state and local laws, rules and regulations applicable to hunting including, but not limited to, manner of take, bag limits, hunting hours, geographical or general implement restrictions, hunting seasons, tagging, transporting and reporting requirements. Section 7. Filing In accordance with ECL Section 11-0935.2, a copy of this Local Law shall be filed with the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation. And in accordance with Municipal Home Rule Section 27, a copy shall be filed with the New York State Department of State. Section 8. Severability Should any provision of this Local Law be declared by the courts to be unconstitutional or invalid, such decision shall not affect the validity of this Local Law as a whole or any part thereof other than the part so determined to be unconstitutional or invalid. Section 9. Repeal All Ordinances, Local Laws and parts thereof inconsistent with this Local Law are hereby repealed. Section 10. Effective Date This Local Law shall be effective upon its filing with the New York State Secretary of State m accordance with Section 27 of the Municipal Home Rule Law.\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature shall conduct a public hearing upon said proposed Local Law at the Legislative Chambers, Niagara County Courthouse, Lockport, New York on the 15th day of June, 2021 at 5:30 p.m., and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Niagara County Legislature, at least six days in advance of such hearing, shall post a notice upon the bulletin boards in the Courthouse at Lockport and the Civic Building at Niagara Falls, and shall publish such notice in the Lockport Union Sun & Journal and the Niagara Gazette; such notice shall contain the title of the Local Law and an abstract of the text to be prepared by the Clerk of the Legislature, with the assistance of the County Attorney's Office.\nApproved for submission.\nMoved by Hill, seconded by Godfrey, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Hill, seconded by Godfrey.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent."};
